  "notes": [
   "The Leonardo is in the catalogue as arduino-leonardo, although the parts list matched it to None.",
   "The joystick breakout's VER pin goes to A0 and its HOR pin to A1 (circuit image and schematic). The tutorial text says x-axis on A0 and y-axis on A1, so VER is mapped to catalogue port Xout and HOR to Yout.",
   "In the image the breadboard is drawn with row labels j-f on the top half and e-a on the bottom half. I followed those labels: the top half is bank 'b' (rows f-j) and the bottom half is bank 't' (rows a-e). This is the reverse of the catalogue's bank naming.",
   "The joystick header pins sit in column 12 and the jumper wire ends in column 11, as drawn. The image shows them touching, so I treated them as connected, although in column terms they are different strips.",
   "Optional status LED on pin 5 (via 220 ohm resistor to GND) is included. Its breadboard placement is my own choice, since the manual gives no layout.",
   "The optional second pushbutton (pin 3, 10k pulldown) is not built because the manual refers to the separate button tutorial for it. The Leonardo's micro-USB cable to the computer is also not modelled.",
   "Pushbutton is integrated in the joystick (SEL pin), so no separate pulldown is used."
  ]
